WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.640
PRIANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to "Get Cooking in Cloud",

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.810
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.250
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.880
we will talk about
creating static websites

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:12.740
on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:22.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.010
Let's say you're a
small company looking

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.380
to setup a basic web presence.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.120
You don't expect
a lot of traffic

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.130
and don't want to pay a
lot to get up and online.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.640
So let's walk through
how to start small,

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.480
deploying a static website.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.740
Static web pages can contain
client side technologies

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.040
such as HTML, CSS,
and JavaScript.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.800
They cannot contain dynamic
content such as service side

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.810
scripts like PHP.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.320
Before you begin, make sure
that you have a Google Cloud

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.080
project created, a
billing account setup,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.110
and you have a domain
that you own or manage.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.390
This should be your
top level domain.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.460
To verify that you
manage the domain,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.860
check the link in the
description below.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.350
Now that you've
covered the logistics,

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.740
there are four steps to
set up static websites.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.950
The first step is to connect
your domain to Google Cloud

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:13.640
Storage.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.980
To do that, create
a CNAME record

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:20.180
that points to
c.storage.googleapis.com.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.200
The second step is to create
a Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.530
To do this, browse to
the Google Cloud console,

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.190
click on Cloud storage,
and click Create Bucket.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.010
Make sure the bucket
name matches the CNAME

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.090
you created for your domain.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.240
In this case, the
storage bucket should

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.550
be named www.example.com.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.060
The third step is
to upload the files

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.280
you want your website to serve.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.870
You can do this in
one of two ways.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.200
First, by directly
uploading the files

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.470
using the Google Cloud
console, or second,

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.900
by using gsutil
command line, which

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.750
is great for when you
have an existing website.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.690
Once the files are
uploaded, accessing them

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.380
is going to be based on how
you choose to share your files.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.870
If you set the permission
at the bucket level,

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:12.740
you can easily and quickly
make every file for your site

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.240
publicly accessible.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.870
For file control, you can
choose to set the permission

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.410
at the object level so that
only specific files are

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.420
accessible through your website.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.930
At this point, you have a
functioning static website,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.160
and you could call it a day.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.200
But we recommend assigning an
index page suffix and a custom

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.970
error page to better
guide your users.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.710
This helps in situations when
a user might request a URL,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.930
but you do not have
an associated file

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.880
with that name in your bucket.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.230
In our example, when there
isn't a file named Apple,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.430
cloud storage will attempt
to serve an index.html.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.280
If that file also doesn't
exist, cloud storage

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.460
will return an error
page with a 404 response.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:03.140
Now the fourth and final step
is to test our static website.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.380
Verify that the content
is served from the bucket

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.080
by requesting the domain
name in a browser.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.010
One important thing to note here
is that Google Cloud Storage

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.230
only supports HTTP.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.580
In order to serve HTTPS and
get all the security goodness,

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.170
you need to either use direct
URIs using a CNAME redirect,

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.100
or migrate to using a load
balancer as a front end.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.820
So today we learned how
to start small and deploy

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.880
a static website on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.660
We used Google Cloud Storage
to host the static content

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.570
and pointed the domain
name to the storage bucket.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.360
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud".

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.660
Here's hoping you can
whip up something great.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.310
Join us next time
because we will

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:53.090
share the recipe to host
websites on managed platform

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.050
Cloud Run.

